WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.984
There are two steps to using functions in JavaScript.

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:17.784
First, we need to define our function, and then we need to call it.

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.944
When you define a new function, you're simply

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:24.548
giving a name to a defined set of actions you want the computer to perform.

00:00:24.548 --> 00:00:29.948
To call a function, you just type the name of the function, followed by parentheses.

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:33.353
This is telling the computer to perform, or execute,

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:36.513
the set of actions you gave a name to.

00:00:36.513 --> 00:00:38.473
You've already been calling functions

00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:39.937
when you use commands that end with

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:45.497
parentheses. The only difference is someone else defined them for you.

00:00:45.497 --> 00:00:49.537
So let's walk through an example together.

00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:49.817
Notice that the

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:53.282
toolbox now has a tab called Functions.

00:00:53.282 --> 00:00:56.762
There are two blocks in there, one for defining a new function

00:00:56.762 --> 00:00:59.082
and one for calling a function.

00:00:59.082 --> 00:00:59.907
Drag a new function

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.147
block out and type in the name you want to give the function.

00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:07.827
Now add the code you want to run when the function is called.

00:01:07.827 --> 00:01:09.891
We'll add a fill command set to red,

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:12.891
a noStroke to remove the outer line,

00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:16.891
and an ellipse command set to a random x and y position.

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:26.696
Hey, we defined a function, but nothing happened.

00:01:26.696 --> 00:01:29.076
The reason is we forgot step 2.

00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:29.856
We need to call the

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.340
function.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.020
To call the function, we drag out the other block

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.660
in the Functions tab and add in the name of our function.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.845
Now that we have both defined and called

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:42.645
our function, the code will actually run, and the dot will appear.

00:01:44.805 --> 00:01:49.605
Defining a function simply gives a name to a block of code without actually running it.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:53.769
The definition can actually be written anywhere in your program.

00:01:53.769 --> 00:01:59.529
The rest of the code will run line by line, just as before.

00:01:59.529 --> 00:01:59.814
Calling a

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:04.054
function runs the lines of code inside the function. Afterwards, the program returns

00:02:04.054 --> 00:02:06.254
to where it left off when the function call was made.

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:15.799
The fact that function definitions can be placed anywhere means that your code can get messy and hard to read.

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:19.783
We typically put function definitions at the end of our programs to help

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:21.783
keep things organized.

00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:29.543
You may have noticed that there's one function you've been defining, but not

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:33.408
calling, in every program you've written so far.

00:02:33.408 --> 00:02:38.768
The draw function is a special function that you as the programmer get to define, but the

00:02:38.768 --> 00:02:39.752
Game Lab

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:42.752
actually takes care of calling for you.

00:02:42.752 --> 00:02:48.452
By calling whatever you've defined as the draw function over and over, 30 times a second,

00:02:48.452 --> 00:02:49.737
Game Lab is able to string lots

00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:56.217
of still images into a smooth animation. Once you have written a procedure or

00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:59.697
function that you know works as intended, you can call the function

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:04.721
as often as you wish without worrying about the details or problems you had to solve to get it working.

00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:11.266
[MUSIC]

00:03:11.266 --> 00:03:15.506
By referencing a large and complex block of code by a single name,

00:03:15.506 --> 00:03:19.666
it makes it much easier to understand what a program is doing by reading the

00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:24.011
code and also easier to locate and fix problems or errors.

00:03:27.451 --> 00:03:28.811
Let's go ahead and dive in.

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:39.675
[MUSIC]
